{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-01-ratio-meaning-units-simplification",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Ratio Meaning, Units, and Simplification",
    "description": "Build ratios from real quantities, simplify them, compare them, and connect part-to-part with part-to-whole.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start with two baskets to show that a ratio is a comparison, not a subtraction. Make units agree before forming a ratio, then simplify and scale ratios. Move through decimal/fraction ratios, ratio comparison, part-to-whole percentages, and whole-number feasibility. Each skill is demonstrated before a fresh near-transfer question.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13, Ratio and Proportion. Approved scope: Teach ratio as a comparison of two quantities in the same units and as the fraction a/b. Cover antecedent and consequent, converting units before comparing, simplifying by a common factor, building equivalent ratios, ratios containing decimals or fractions, comparing two ratios by cross-products, converting part-to-part ratios into part-to-whole fractions or percentages, and checking whether a proposed total can fit a whole-number ratio. Use concrete counts, money, time and length. Learners should know exactly what each term represents before calculating."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A ratio is a comparison, not just two numbers with a colon between them.\n\nWe’ll build ratios from real quantities, make the units match, simplify them mentally, and connect part-to-part with part-to-whole.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's start with two baskets.\n\nBasket A has 12 apples. Basket B has 18.\n\nThe ratio A : B asks, ‘for every how many in A, how many are in B?’",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "12 : 18 has a common factor of 6.\n\n12 : 18 = 2 : 3\n\nSo every 2 apples in A match 3 in B. Dividing both terms by the same non-zero number keeps the comparison unchanged.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
      "content": {
        "text": "Simplify 18 : 30.",
